  "data": {
    "query": "Powell Jackson Hole August 2019 trade policy uncertainty business investment",
    "search_window": {
      "from": "2019-07-29",
      "to": "2019-08-28"
    },
    "sources": {
      "grounding": {
        "generic": [
          {
            "url": "https://www.usnews.com/news/economy/articles/2019-08-23/powell-dodges-trade-trump-in-jackson-hole",
            "title": "Powell Dodges Trade, Trump in Jackson Hole",
            "snippets": [
              "# Powell Dodges Trade, Trump in Jackson Hole\nTrump was quick to hit back at the Fed chairman after Powell said monetary policy can't set a \"rulebook for international trade.\"\n\n News & World Report\n\nPowell: Winning Trade War Not Fed's Job",
              "Federal Reserve Chairman Jerome Powell and his colleagues on the Federal Open Market Committee left their benchmark interest rate unchanged following their January meeting.(Annie Rice/AP)\n\nFederal Reserve Chairman Jerome Powell on Friday acknowledged the Fed is limited in how it can respond to an ongoing trade war with China, noting that monetary policy is a \"powerful\" but not all-purpose tool",
              "During a speech at the central bank's annual monetary policy conference in Jackson Hole, Wyoming, Powell stopped short of directly criticizing President Donald Trump or acknowledging the pressure Trump has heaped on him to lower interest rates to help support the administration's battle with Beijing.\n\nInstead, he touched on the Fed's long-standing dual mandate of promoting full employment and fighting inflation. Navigating a conflict with China, he said, isn't something that has historically been the Fed's job.\n\n[\n\nREAD:",
              "China Targets Soybeans, Automobiles and Oil in Latest Tariffs ]\n\n\"Setting trade policy is the business of Congress and the administration, not that of the Fed,\" Powell said Friday. \"Moreover, while monetary policy is a powerful tool that works to support consumer spending, business investment and public confidence, it cannot provide a settled rulebook for international trade.\"",
              "Powell's comments came just hours after Chinese officials announced a slew of new tariffs on $75 billion of American exports. Powell acknowledged during his speech that the trade uncertainty surrounding Trump's administration \"seems to be playing a role in the global economic slowdown and in weak manufacturing and capital spending in the United States.\"\n\nShortly after Powell's remarks, the president tweeted that he wondered \"who is our bigger enemy, Jay Powell or Chairman Xi,\" referring to Chinese President Xi Jinping",
              "\"Tensions between the president and the Fed will add to economic uncertainty,\" Gus Faucher, a senior vice president and chief economist at The PNC Financial Services Group, wrote in a research note Friday, noting that stocks plummeted shortly after the president's tweets were published",
              "Trump went on to tweet that the U.S. would be \"better off without\" China and that \"American companies are hereby ordered to immediately start looking for an alternative to China.\" The Dow Jones Industrial Average, which had held relatively steady earlier in the morning, was down nearly 400 points half an hour after the tweet was published.\n\nBy mid-afternoon, the Dow was down more than 500 points.\n\n[\n\nMORE:",
              "Trumps’ Statements Loom Large Over the Fed’s Decisions ]\n\nIt is this ongoing trade and China uncertainty, however, that may force Powell into reducing interest rates further at the Federal Open Market Committee's next two-day gathering. Powell in late July announced the Fed's first rate cut since 2008, and analysts widely expect at least one more reduction – though perhaps not to the scale that Trump would want – by the end of the year",
              "\"Clearly Powell was in favor of the rate cut last month and is in favor of another rate cut next month, saying 'We will act as appropriate to sustain the expansion' and 'risk management enters our decision making,'\" Chris Zaccarelli, chief investment officer of Independent Advisor Alliance, said in a statement Friday",
              "Although Powell passed the buck on supporting the president's trade war, the degree to which the Trump administration takes action against China – thereby exposing the economy to additional risk in the midst of the longest economic expansion in U.S. history – may influence how drastically he acts on the interest rate front in the months ahead"
            ]
          },
          {
            "url": "https://www.cnbc.com/2019/08/23/powell-jackson-hole-speech.html",
            "title": "Fed Chairman Jerome Powell Jackson Hole speech: No 'rulebook' on trade",
            "snippets": [
              "Powell repeats his pledge to keep the economic expansion going while acknowledging that tariffs and other factors are causing growth to slow.\nlogo\nBusiness\nTech\nVideo\nPRO",
              "# Powell says there's no 'rulebook' for trade war, pledges to 'act as appropriate' to sustain economy\n- Federal Reserve Chairman Jerome Powell says the global economic outlook \"has been deteriorating.\"\n- In his annual remarks at Jackson Hole, he again pledged to \"act as appropriate to sustain the expansion,\" a key phrase he has used before and has prompted positive market reaction.\n- He spoke at length about tariffs, saying there is no \"rulebook\" on trade wars and that the Fed may have to \"look through\" short-range developments",
              "- But Powell also says the \"economy is close to both goals\" of price stability and full employment",
              "Powell: The Fed will act as appropriate to sustain economic expansion\n\nwatch now\n\nVIDEO 2:54 02:54\n\nPowell: The Fed will act as appropriate to sustain economic expansion\n\nSquawk on the Street\n\nFederal Reserve Chairman Jerome Powell repeated his pledge Friday to keep the economic expansion going while acknowledging that tariffs and other factors are causing growth to slow. Less than an hour after the speech, President Donald Trump blasted Powell on Twitter, referring to him as \"our enemy.\"",
              "Powell, while not saying specifically where he thought rates should go, promised that the Fed \"will act as appropriate to sustain the expansion,\" a phrase he has used several times in the recent past.\n\nPowell also said in his annual remarks at the central bank's Jackson Hole symposium that the \"economy is close to both goals\" of the Fed's dual mandate of full employment and price stability",
              "\"Our challenge now is to do what monetary policy can do to sustain the expansion so that the benefits of the strong jobs market extend to more of those still left behind, and so that inflation is centered firmly around 2 percent.\"\n\nHe also outlined the challenges the Fed faces and indicated that for he and his fellow officials there are \"no recent precedents to guide any policy response to the current situation.\"",
              "\"While monetary policy is a powerful tool that works to support consumer spending, business investment, and public confidence, it cannot provide a settled rulebook for international trade,\" he said in prepared remarks. \"We can, however, try to look through what may be passing events, focus on how trade developments are affecting the outlook, and adjust policy to promote our objectives.\"\n\nHe did say the Fed is looking at ways to address developments in a landscape that has changed significantly since the expansion began a decade ago",
              "\"We are examining the monetary policy tools we have used both in calm times and in crisis, and we are asking whether we should expand our toolkit,\" he said.",
              "On a broader level, Powell painted a mostly positive picture on the U.S. economy, saying it has \"continued to perform well overall\" while facing challenges.\n\n\"The global growth outlook has been deteriorating since the middle of last year. Trade policy uncertainty seems to be playing a role in the global slowdown and in weak manufacturing and capital spending in the United States,\" he said",
              "At its July 30-31 meeting, the policymaking Federal Open Market Committee approved a 25 basis point rate cut, the first reduction in 11 years. Officials cited concerns over weaker global growth, the U.S.-China trade tensions and low inflation in making the reduction.\n\nMarkets expect the Fed to approve another cut at its September meeting and another before the end of the year",
              "In his discussion Friday, Powell broke Fed policy into three eras: one that lasted from 1950-82 that saw the Fed use \"stop and go\" policy that promoted faster growth but that ultimately ended up with runaway inflation; 1983-2009, in which the Fed controlled inflation but saw financial excesses that culminated in the Great Recession; and the current era, in which policymakers are confronting a world of slower growth with lower-than-normal interest rates and",
              "\"Because the most important effects of monetary policy are felt with uncertain lags of a year or more, the Committee must attempt to look through what may be passing developments and focus on things that seem likely to affect the outlook over time or that pose a material risk of doing so,\" he added. \"But fitting trade policy uncertainty into this framework is a new challenge. Setting trade policy is the business of Congress and the Administration, not that of the Fed.\"",
              "Powell did not mention the yield curve inversion that has been rattling the markets, in which the 2-year Treasury note has surpassed the 10-year, a reliable recession indicator for the past 50 years. Minutes from the last meeting also offered only passing reference to the curve spread, and Powell made no mention of recession in his speech.\n\nTrump thundered Powell on Twitter, saying \"the Fed did NOTHING.\"",
